recently Australia got to see a total  lunar eclipse this is when the earth  gets between the Sun and the moon and  the shadow of the earth is cast on the  moon turning it dark and red I was able  to go and see the lunar eclipse with  some of my friends and it actually got  me thinking about the topic of being an  expert was on something or at least  being perceived as an expert so back and  I guess a previous life of mine when I  was an undergrad in New Zealand I was  involved in an urban astronomy group we  would take telescopes out onto the  street and I would show members of the  public through the telescope and answer  their questions about space and about  astronomy I guess during that time I  would have been expected to be an expert  on things like a lunar eclipse phases of  the Moon and how all of that is working  and being in that role gave me a lot of  confidence to answer questions on that  topic even if I didn't totally  understand them I felt a level of  confidence - I guess Reis a explanations  that I'd heard other people give or to  say something that I knew made physical  sense enough so that it would satisfy me  and satisfy the person asking the  question even if I did have some of my  deeper like questions myself about how  this was working I wouldn't really raise  them because I was expected to be in the  role of answering the questions not  asking them when I went up to watch the  Eclipse the other week I didn't really  tell these friends I was with about that  you know experience that I had with  telescopes and stuff and I just sort of  took a backseat role of letting someone  else in the group stand up as that  expert and answer our questions about  how the Eclipse was working because I  still had things I didn't totally  understand and that I wanted to know  more about so I found viewing the  Eclipse to be really fun because I was  able to ask questions things like you  know how much darker is the moon going  to get and how long is it going to laugh  and which countries can see it right now  and I don't think I would have felt  confident asking those questions if I  had been in the role of the expected  expert I think it was only yeah from  that backseat role I was able to really  engage my own curiosity instead of  standing on I guess a pedestal and just  regurgitating explanations that I had  sort of heard or read on the internet or  something and I don't know when this  sort of thing started but it's  definitely something I've faced a lot in  my life as being that expected expert in  a topic like way back in high school  people who knew in my class and my  teachers that I had quite an interest in  physics and astronomy and even back then  like the teachers would maybe pick on me  to answer a question about some obscure  astronomy thing and I probably wouldn't  know the answer but I would feel like I  had to like I was responsible for  knowing all the physics and astronomy at  that age even though that's quite absurd  for anyone to ever know all of the  subject I would feel that just because  my peers and my class expected me to be  tobe the person there knows stuff about  physics and astronomy that if I didn't  know the answer it was sort of shameful  and that I didn't really feel  comfortable as much as I should have  asking other people in my class  Christians about physics because I was  the one who was expected to be the  expert on this and not them and it's  quite a dangerous mindset to just stop  your own questions in your own curiosity  because you are suddenly expected to  know more about it than other people  like even going on through uni and then  I graduated with a physics degree it  still sort of hampers me in a way  because when I'm with people I'm afraid  that if I ask too many perceived dumb  questions about physics they will look  at me and think like how did you get  your physics degree like as if I have a  lack of confidence and the worthiness of  my physics work and of my physics degree  that if I ask too many damn questions I  didn't deserve it  and like I said I think it's quite  damaging to be in that role like I do  have the degree which means I do know  some stuff about physics but I still  think it's absurd for anyone to think  that they know all of physics and there  are going to be mistakes that I make in  explanations and things I don't  understand but yeah I feel like I need  to gain more confidence in asking the  questions despite my qualifications  I think someone with the qualification  who holds that up has meaning that you  should trust them and everything they  say is a bad thing because your  qualification shouldn't really give you  so much  superiority over other people it should  represent that you have at least proven  you know a base level of things but I  don't think there's anything that really  proves that you should trust every word  someone says I think you should always  and a question people with  qualifications and people with  qualifications should ask questions of  everyone else I get how saying this  could also be damaging in the other  direction like you you want people who  do have levels of understanding to  sometimes be the ones to stand up and  tell people how something works and you  want people to believe in scientists and  believe in people who have gone through  like all the education system and then  actually do you know what they're  talking about because a lot of problems  and science come when people don't deny  facts that scientists try to tell them  and there's this whole mistrust and  science going on so it can be damaging  to say like people with qualifications  shouldn't act like they know everything  because sometimes that confidence is  what they need to do to get important  ideas across but I don't really know the  answer here like I think it's a bit of  both if you're going to have people  without qualifications and without the  education standing up and being the ones  who are confident enough to say listen  to me this is how something works and  you've got all the scientists standing  on the other hand say  ah you know we we're not quite sure in  the science and we're still learning as  well we make mistakes and admitting the  truth and the honesty behind science  then that's going to lead to a really  skewed reality where I guess the wrong  people come into power so I don't think  scientists should be totally like  unconfident and what they're trying to  get across but I think that scientists  and people with any kind of interest or  you know perceived expert level and  something should continue asking  questions and should continue engaging  with people and not let themselves think  that they are on this pedestal where  they are only there to answer questions  and not to ask them as well I'd love to  hear your thoughts on this because it's  not really something I've heard people  talking about before but it's just  something that crossed my mind the other  week when I felt like for the first time  in a while that I could really just ask  questions without being judged so leave  a comment below if you've ever felt a  similar thing to this and I'll see you  next time
 

